W5 is complete. I changed only [scoring.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/scoring.py), [tasks/build_task.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tasks/build_task.py), [tests/test_scoring.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/test_scoring.py), [tests/test_task_build.py](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/test_task_build.py), and the two mini JSONL fixtures in [tests/data](/Users/sjh/Dev.to%20Challenges/challenges/kaggle-benchmarking-2026/tests/data). I did not commit or install packages.

**Verification:** `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_scoring.py tests/test_task_build.py -q` → **16 passed in 0.55s**. The generated task compiled and returned 1.0 with a perfect stub LLM and 0.0 with a garbage-response stub.

**Installed SDK 0.6.1 facts:** `@kbench.task` accepts `name` and `store_task`; `llm.prompt` accepts `temperature` and `extra_api_params`, but has no dedicated token-cap argument. The builder sends a 1024-token cap through `extra_api_params`, using the parameter name for the SDK’s Google GenAI or OpenAI path. `Task.evaluate` exists, but the generated task uses a sequential loop. The package imported offline; I found no built-in usable mock LLM, so execution testing used the requested fake SDK and stub. The SDK normalizes `Gone for Good?` to `Gone_for_Good`, so the generated decorator uses `gone-for-good` to meet the required slug.

**Assumptions and remaining work:** The generated task uses raw text responses so the scorer can record format failures and the raw response. The main `data/gold.jsonl` and `data/items.jsonl` were still being built by W1, so I did not generate the final Kaggle task file or run against real models.